Hi,
   I am trying to bootstrap myself into the Gentoo Alpha Linux 
distribution, on a "half-flash" LX164 running AlphaBios/Milo. (The Milo 
is that built against a 2.0-vintage kernel.) With the boot command

boot sr:boot/vmlinuz initrd=initrd.img root=/dev/ram0 init=/linuxrc

a la the Gentoo handbook, the kernel appears to at least partly load, 
and then the whole business chokes when attempting to mount the root 
partition--that is, there are some complaints from the FAT and XFS 
subsystems (I guess as the kernel tries on the different filesystem 
types for the specified device?) ending in a kernel panic from VFS that 
it is unable to mount the root partition on device 01:00. I've checked 
that the various /dev/ram. devices are on the CD. (Curiously, the 
/dev/ram device is symlinked to /dev/ram1, rather than ram0--however, 
none of /dev/ram,/dev/ram0,/dev/ram1 give any different result.) The 
Gentoo approach seems like such a great way to do things, I would like 
to see this succeed...Any suggestions on what to try?

Thanks in advance,
Eric Henry
eric@helix.nih.gov


