Thats a shame. I thought these were EV4 practices (luckyly me, my EV4
were all full flashed and I heard of the half flashed systems years later).
I didn't believe they did this also on EV5 systems so far...

>I downloaded the Tru64 dmcc21164_v2_8.exe from
>ran "update -srm". This time I was asked if I really want to alter the
>firmware and I chose yes.

Update -srm does only update the SRM (which is what you wanted).
Using "Update" means the program tries to find what else could be
updated (like disk controllers, network controllers) and requires
a datafile with a list of possible devices and the related f/w filenames.
You don't need them really so the missing file message can be ignored now.

You might later decide to do the upgrade again (from CD) and look for
other possible f/w upgrades.

>The report of the update was successfully and the comment told me to power
>cycle the machine, I switched it off, waited some 20 seconds and switched
>it on again. Since then it doens't boot, in fact the monitor doesn't even
>get a signal.

Maybe the update was successful and SRM started, but on the serial and
not on the graphic console. Type "SET CONSOLE GRAPHICS" blind (and repower)
or better connect a terminal to the serial line to prove me right or wrong.

HIH

